1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:13,886
This is MATH 239, video 5 a. The topic is the Principle of Inclusion and Exclusion, from Section 1.1.6 of the notes, and its use for derangements, from Section 1.2.5.

2
00:00:14,336 --> 00:00:28,685
After this video, you can count a union of overlapping sets with Theorem 1.15. You can explain why the formula counts each element once. You can count the elements that are in none of the sets. And you can count surjections and derangements.

3
00:00:29,135 --> 00:00:44,786
Section 1.1.6 starts with this problem. Each flower in a bouquet is fresh, fragrant, or colourful, or more than one of these. We know seven counts, a to g. How many flowers are in the bouquet?

4
00:00:45,236 --> 00:00:59,771
Draw one circle for each property. A flower is inside each circle for a property that it has. Each flower is in at least one circle. The circles overlap, because one flower can have two or three properties.

5
00:01:00,221 --> 00:01:10,939
So we cannot just add 11, 7 and 8. The sum 26 counts a flower in two circles two times, and a flower in all three circles three times. The red labels show this.

6
00:01:11,389 --> 00:01:20,150
Example 1.14 counts each region of the diagram. Start at the centre. Item g says that 2 flowers have all three properties.

7
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:37,654
Next, the regions with exactly two properties. Item f says 2 flowers are fragrant and colourful. The 2 flowers in the centre are part of them. So 2 minus 2, which is 0, are fragrant and colourful but not fresh. The same step gives 3 and 4 for the other two regions.

8
00:01:38,104 --> 00:01:56,308
Now the regions with one property only. The colourful circle has 8 flowers. Four regions make up this circle. Subtract the three known regions: 8 minus 2 minus 0 minus 3 is 3. In the same way, we get 1 fragrant only flower and 2 fresh only flowers.

9
00:01:56,758 --> 00:02:04,328
The seven regions do not overlap, and together they make the bouquet. So the total is their sum, which is 15 flowers.

10
00:02:04,778 --> 00:02:24,329
The notes then give a shorter calculation. Add the three single counts. Subtract the three pair counts. Add the triple count. 11 plus 7 plus 8, minus 6, 5 and 2, plus 2, is 15. This is the same answer. Here is why it works. We follow how many times each region is counted.

11
00:02:24,779 --> 00:02:36,203
Add a, b and c. A region with one property is counted 1 time. A region with two properties is counted 2 times. The centre is counted 3 times.

12
00:02:36,653 --> 00:02:48,403
Subtract d, e and f. A region with two properties is in exactly one pair set, so it drops to 1. The centre is in all three pair sets, so it drops from 3 to 0.

13
00:02:48,853 --> 00:03:04,781
Add g. Only the centre is in the triple set, so it goes back to 1. Now every region is counted exactly once. So the short formula gives the number of flowers. With more sets, the regions are hard to draw. The short formula still works.

14
00:03:05,231 --> 00:03:24,504
Now the general theorem. Let A 1 to A m be finite sets. For a nonempty set S of indices, A sub S is the intersection of the sets A i with i in S. For example, A sub the set 2, 3, 5 is A 2 intersect A 3 intersect A 5.

15
00:03:24,954 --> 00:03:43,948
For the flowers, m is 3. Let A 1 be the fresh flowers, A 2 the fragrant flowers, and A 3 the colourful flowers. The sets S of size 1 give A 1, A 2 and A 3, with 11, 7 and 8 flowers. Their sign is plus.

16
00:03:44,398 --> 00:03:51,643
The sets S of size 2 give the pair intersections, with 6, 5 and 2 flowers. Their sign is minus.

17
00:03:52,093 --> 00:03:57,712
The set 1, 2, 3 gives the triple intersection, with 2 flowers. Its sign is plus again.

18
00:03:58,162 --> 00:04:16,041
Theorem 1.15 says: the size of the union is the sum over all nonempty S of minus 1 to the size of S minus 1, times the size of A sub S. There are 2 to the m minus 1 terms. Odd sizes get a plus sign. Even sizes get a minus sign.

19
00:04:16,491 --> 00:04:29,773
Why does the theorem work for any m? Take one element x. Say x is in exactly k of the sets. Then x is in A sub S exactly when every index in S is one of those k indices.

20
00:04:30,223 --> 00:04:42,112
Take k equal to 3: x is in A 1, A 2 and A 3. The three sets S of size 1 each count x once, with a plus sign. That gives plus 3.

21
00:04:42,562 --> 00:04:49,063
The three sets S of size 2 each count x once, with a minus sign. That gives minus 3.

22
00:04:49,513 --> 00:04:57,640
The set 1, 2, 3 counts x once more, with a plus sign. The total is 3 minus 3 plus 1, which is 1.

23
00:04:58,090 --> 00:05:23,261
For any k, there are k choose j sets S of size j. So x is counted the sum of minus 1 to the j minus 1, times k choose j, times. By the Binomial Theorem, this is 1 minus the quantity 1 minus 1 to the k. That is 1, because k is at least 1. The rows show k from 1 to 5. Section 2.5 of the notes gives the full proof.

24
00:05:23,711 --> 00:05:34,206
An element that is in none of the sets is in no A sub S. So it is counted zero times. Thus the sum counts each element of the union exactly once.

25
00:05:34,656 --> 00:05:46,963
Many problems ask for the objects with none of some bad properties. Put all the sets inside one set U of all objects. The green region is the set of objects in none of the sets A i.

26
00:05:47,413 --> 00:06:05,617
Its size is the size of U minus the size of the union. Apply Theorem 1.15 to the union, and change all the signs. If we agree that A sub the empty set is U, then we get one sum over all subsets S, with the sign minus 1 to the size of S.

27
00:06:06,067 --> 00:06:19,509
So the plan is: name each bad property as a set A i. Count every intersection A sub S. Then subtract with inclusion and exclusion. The derangement problem later in this video uses exactly this form.

28
00:06:19,959 --> 00:06:28,969
Here is an example to practise. How many integers from 1 to 100 are divisible by at least one of 2, 3 and 5? Pause the video and try it.

29
00:06:29,419 --> 00:06:37,360
Let A 1 be the multiples of 2. Every second number is in A 1, so A 1 has 50 elements. The blue dots mark them.

30
00:06:37,810 --> 00:06:47,980
Let A 2 be the multiples of 3. There are 33 of them, because 3 times 33 is 99. The purple dots mark them.

31
00:06:48,430 --> 00:06:57,718
Let A 3 be the multiples of 5. There are 20 of them. The orange dots mark them. Some cells have two or three dots. These are the overlaps.

32
00:06:58,168 --> 00:07:16,094
A number is divisible by 2 and by 3 exactly when it is divisible by 6. So A 1 intersect A 2 has 16 elements. In the same way, multiples of 10 give 10, and multiples of 15 give 6. Multiples of 30 give 3.

33
00:07:16,544 --> 00:07:30,801
By inclusion and exclusion, the answer is 50 plus 33 plus 20, minus 16, 10 and 6, plus 3. That is 74. The green cells are the 74 numbers. The 26 gray cells have no dot.

34
00:07:31,251 --> 00:07:40,725
One more short example, with only two sets. How many binary strings of length 8 start with 1 1 or end with 0 0? Pause the video and try it.

35
00:07:41,175 --> 00:07:53,418
Let A 1 be the strings that start with 1 1. Two positions are fixed. Each of the other 6 positions is 0 or 1. So A 1 has 2 to the 6, which is 64, strings.

36
00:07:53,868 --> 00:08:00,648
Let A 2 be the strings that end with 0 0. By the same reasoning, it also has 64 strings.

37
00:08:01,098 --> 00:08:16,191
A string in both sets has four fixed positions, so the intersection has 2 to the 4, which is 16, strings. With two sets, the theorem gives 64 plus 64 minus 16. The answer is 112.

38
00:08:16,641 --> 00:08:31,502
This is practice problem P1-7 from Assignment 1. A has three elements, a, b and c. B has five elements. Count all functions from A to B, the injective ones, and the surjective ones. Pause the video and try it.

39
00:08:31,952 --> 00:08:47,788
Part a. A function gives each element of A one value in B. Draw one arrow from each element of A. Each of the three arrows has 5 choices. By the AND rule, there are 5 cubed, which is 125, functions.

40
00:08:48,238 --> 00:09:03,377
Part b. An injective function gives different elements different values. The arrow from a has 5 choices. Then the arrow from b has 4 choices, and the arrow from c has 3. So there are 60 injective functions.

41
00:09:03,827 --> 00:09:19,384
Part c. A surjective function reaches every element of B. But there are only 3 arrows, so at most 3 elements of B get an arrow. At least two elements, here in red, get no arrow. So there are 0 surjective functions.

42
00:09:19,834 --> 00:09:43,472
Inclusion and exclusion also counts surjections when the answer is not zero. Count the surjections from a set of 4 elements to the set 1, 2, 3. Pause the video and try it. Let U be all functions. There are 3 to the 4, which is 81. Let A i be the functions with no arrow to i. A function is surjective when it is in none of the sets A i.

43
00:09:43,922 --> 00:10:01,987
Take a set S of k values. A function in A sub S uses only the other 3 minus k values. Each of the 4 elements has 3 minus k choices. So the size of A sub S is 3 minus k to the 4. It depends only on k, and there are 3 choose k such sets S.

44
00:10:02,437 --> 00:10:19,992
Fill the table. For k equal to 1: 3 sets, 16 functions each, so minus 48. For k equal to 2: 3 sets, 1 function each, so plus 3. For k equal to 3: no value is left, so the term is 0.

45
00:10:20,442 --> 00:10:31,169
The sum is 81 minus 48 plus 3, which is 36. A list of all 81 functions by computer also gives 36 surjections.

46
00:10:31,619 --> 00:10:39,839
As a check, use the same method for P1-7 part c. Now the domain has 3 elements and B has 5 elements.

47
00:10:40,289 --> 00:10:47,998
The terms are minus 1 to the k, times 5 choose k, times 5 minus k cubed, for k from 0 to 5.

48
00:10:48,448 --> 00:11:01,591
125 minus 320 plus 270 minus 80 plus 5 minus 0 is 0. This agrees with the direct argument. Use the direct argument on an exam when it is shorter.

49
00:11:02,041 --> 00:11:16,437
Now the classical application in Section 1.2.5. Eight people check their coats at a restaurant. The clerk returns the coats in a random order. What is the probability that no person gets the correct coat? We solve it for any number n of people.

50
00:11:16,887 --> 00:11:34,531
List the people P 1 to P n. Record the result as a sequence c 1 to c n. c i equals j means that person i got the coat of person j. Each number from 1 to n appears exactly once. So the sequence is a permutation of 1 to n.

51
00:11:34,981 --> 00:11:48,820
Person i gets the correct coat exactly when c i equals i. Here c 2 equals 2, so person 2 is lucky. A permutation with c i different from i for every i is a derangement.

52
00:11:49,270 --> 00:12:04,549
Let A i be the set of permutations with c i equal to i. The union of the A i is the set where at least one person gets the correct coat. So the number of derangements is n factorial minus the size of the union. This is the complement form.

53
00:12:04,999 --> 00:12:22,321
We need the size of every A sub S. The notes use the example n equals 8 and S equal to the set 2, 3, 6. A permutation in A sub S has 2 in position 2, 3 in position 3, and 6 in position 6. The stars are the free positions.

54
00:12:22,771 --> 00:12:32,198
The numbers 1, 4, 5, 7 and 8 fill the five free positions in some order. Each order is a list of these five numbers. Here is one of them in green.

55
00:12:32,648 --> 00:12:39,939
There are 5 factorial lists of five numbers. So A sub the set 2, 3, 6 has 120 permutations.

56
00:12:40,389 --> 00:12:58,687
In general, if S has k elements, fix the k positions in S. The other n minus k numbers form a list in the other positions. So the size of A sub S is n minus k factorial. It depends only on k, and there are n choose k sets S of size k.

57
00:12:59,137 --> 00:13:13,487
Now apply Theorem 1.15. Group the sets S by their size k. Each of the n choose k sets of size k gives the same term: minus 1 to the k minus 1, times n minus k factorial.

58
00:13:13,937 --> 00:13:36,832
Simplify n choose k times n minus k factorial. n choose k is n factorial over k factorial times n minus k factorial. The factor n minus k factorial cancels. What is left is n factorial over k factorial. So the union has n factorial times the sum of minus 1 to the k minus 1 over k factorial.

59
00:13:37,282 --> 00:13:48,984
Subtract from n factorial. Each sign changes. The result is n factorial times the sum, for k from 0 to n, of minus 1 to the k over k factorial.

60
00:13:49,434 --> 00:13:55,239
The k equal to 0 term is n factorial. It is the term for the empty set S, which is all of U.

61
00:13:55,689 --> 00:14:05,117
Check with n equal to 4. The terms are 24, minus 24, plus 12, minus 4, plus 1. The sum is 9.

62
00:14:05,567 --> 00:14:14,576
A computer list of all 24 permutations of 1, 2, 3, 4 finds exactly these 9 derangements. In each one, no number is in its own position.

63
00:14:15,026 --> 00:14:34,190
All n factorial permutations are equally likely. So the probability of a derangement is the count over n factorial. This is D n, the sum of minus 1 to the k over k factorial, for k from 0 to n. The table shows some exact values. The blue points show D n for n from 0 to 10.

64
00:14:34,640 --> 00:14:50,987
The sum is the start of the series for e to the x, at x equal to minus 1. So D n approaches 1 over e, about 0.367879. The purple line marks this value. From n equal to 7 on, D n changes very little.

65
00:14:51,437 --> 00:15:06,390
For the eight people of Example 1.23, the probability is 2119 over 5760. That is about 0.367882, or close to 36.79 percent.

66
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:18,404
A last practice question. How many lists of 1 to 5 have 1 in position 1, or 2 in position 2, or 3 in position 3? Pause the video and try it.

67
00:15:18,854 --> 00:15:35,061
Let A i be the lists with i in position i. Fix 1 in position 1. The other four numbers form a list of the other four positions, so A 1 has 4 factorial, which is 24, lists. A 2 and A 3 also have 24.

68
00:15:35,511 --> 00:15:43,128
Fix two positions. Three numbers are free, so each pair intersection has 3 factorial, which is 6, lists.

69
00:15:43,578 --> 00:15:51,333
Fix all three positions. Two numbers are free, so the triple intersection has 2 factorial, which is 2, lists.

70
00:15:51,783 --> 00:16:10,591
Here is the written solution. Define the sets A i. Give the size of A sub S with a reason. Name the theorem. Then 3 times 24, minus 3 times 6, plus 2, is 56. A computer list of all 120 lists also finds 56.

71
00:16:11,041 --> 00:16:24,416
To recap. When sets overlap, the sum of their sizes counts shared elements more than once. Theorem 1.15 corrects this with alternating signs. Each element of the union is counted exactly once.

72
00:16:24,866 --> 00:16:36,522
To count the objects in none of the sets, use the complement form, with A sub the empty set equal to U. When the size of A sub S depends only on the size k, group the terms with m choose k.

73
00:16:36,972 --> 00:16:51,880
This gives the count of surjections, and the count of derangements. The probability of a derangement approaches 1 over e. The next video, 5 b, uses counting to find probabilities: birthdays, Wine Gums and poker hands.
